SOAR Tool Integration
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Install and configure open-source SOAR tools (TheHive and Cortex)
Integrate SOAR platforms with Wazuh SIEM for automated incident response
Create automated playbooks for common security incidents
Implement workflow orchestration for security event handling
Test and validate automated response actions
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line proficiency
Understanding of security incident response concepts
Familiarity with SIEM systems and log analysis
Basic Python scripting knowledge
Understanding of JSON and YAML formats
Lab Environment
This lab uses a pre-configured Ubuntu 20.04 LTS cloud machine with Docker and required dependencies installed. Click Start Lab to access your environment. All services will run in Docker containers for easy management and isolation.

Task 1: Install and Configure SOAR Components
Step 1: Update System and Verify Docker
# Update system packages
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Verify Docker installation
docker --version
docker-compose --version

# Ensure Docker service is running
sudo systemctl status docker
Step 2: Deploy Wazuh SIEM
# Create Wazuh directory
mkdir -p ~/wazuh-soar-lab && cd ~/wazuh-soar-lab

# Download Wazuh Docker Compose configuration
curl -so docker-compose.yml https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wazuh/wazuh-docker/v4.7.0/single-node/docker-compose.yml

# Start Wazuh services
docker-compose up -d

# Wait for services to initialize (5-10 minutes)
sleep 300

# Verify services are running
docker-compose ps
Note: Default Wazuh credentials are admin:admin. Access the web interface at https://localhost:443.

Step 3: Deploy TheHive Case Management Platform
# Create TheHive directory
mkdir -p ~/thehive-soar && cd ~/thehive-soar

# Create Docker Compose configuration
cat > docker-compose.yml << 'EOF'
version: '3.8'
services:
  elasticsearch:
    image: docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:7.17.9
    environment:
      - discovery.type=single-node
      - cluster.name=hive
      - xpack.security.enabled=false
      - "ES_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms256m -Xmx256m"
    ports:
      - "9200:9200"
    volumes:
      - es_data:/usr/share/elasticsearch/data

  thehive:
    image: thehiveproject/thehive4:4.1.24-1
    depends_on:
      - elasticsearch
    ports:
      - "9000:9000"
    volumes:
      - thehive_data:/opt/thp/thehive/data
    command: '--no-config --no-config-secret'

volumes:
  es_data:
  thehive_data:
EOF

# Start TheHive services
docker-compose up -d

# Wait for initialization
sleep 120

# Check service status
docker-compose ps
Note: Access TheHive at http://localhost:9000. Initial setup will prompt for admin user creation.

Step 4: Deploy Cortex Analysis Engine
# Create Cortex directory
mkdir -p ~/cortex-soar && cd ~/cortex-soar

# Create Docker Compose configuration
cat > docker-compose.yml << 'EOF'
version: '3.8'
services:
  elasticsearch:
    image: docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:7.17.9
    environment:
      - discovery.type=single-node
      - cluster.name=cortex
      - xpack.security.enabled=false
      - "ES_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms256m -Xmx256m"
    ports:
      - "9201:9200"
    volumes:
      - cortex_es_data:/usr/share/elasticsearch/data

  cortex:
    image: thehiveproject/cortex:3.1.7-1
    depends_on:
      - elasticsearch
    ports:
      - "9001:9001"
    volumes:
      - cortex_jobs:/tmp/cortex-jobs
    environment:
      - 'JOB_DIRECTORY=/tmp/cortex-jobs'

volumes:
  cortex_es_data:
  cortex_jobs:
EOF

# Start Cortex services
docker-compose up -d

# Wait for initialization
sleep 90

# Verify deployment
docker-compose ps
Note: Access Cortex at http://localhost:9001.

